+# SWA STORE STRIDE -- disk budget for hybrid (full + sliding-window) models.
+#
+# The alignment store filter keeps each sliding-window group's tail (and the
+# eagle group's segment head) at EVERY full-attention boundary, so that any of
+# them can be a load point. On DeepSeek V4 (bpc=8) that is, per 2048 tokens,
+# 7 chunk files of 8.6 MB: g0 x1, g1 x1, g2 x2, g3 x1, g4 x2 -- the four SWA
+# groups are 6/7 of the tier. Measured on agent traffic: ~50 GB/h, ~1.2 TB/day.
+#
+# Almost none of the interior boundaries ever become load points. With stride
+# S the SWA groups keep their tail/head only at boundaries that are multiples
+# of S * alignment tokens, PLUS the last two boundaries of every request's
+# offloadable prefix -- where a multi-turn client's next request resumes. Only
+# prompt tokens are offloaded by default (offload_prompt_only), so that end is
+# known when the chunk is stored. (With offload_prompt_only off it moves with
+# decode, so decode-region boundaries are kept as well: more stores, still
+# correct.) Full-attention groups are untouched.
+#
+# The load path is unchanged: _sliding_window_lookup scans backward for the
+# last stored window below the ceiling, every SWA group lands on the same
+# boundary, and the eagle pair (tail of the segment ending there + head of the
+# segment starting there) is kept at exactly those boundaries, so the
+# admissible sets still coincide and the lookup converges in one pass. Chunks
+# already on disk from stride 1 remain valid (a superset).
+#
+# Measured, S=8: ~25% of the disk bytes; resuming a conversation re-prefills the
+# same as stride 1 (simulated 3000 turns: median 1533 vs 1536 tokens, 0
+# uncertified loads). Without the prompt-end boundaries every resumed turn paid
+# up to 14k tokens (~9 s) of extra prefill -- do not drop them.
+#   1 / unset = stock filter.
+SWA_STORE_STRIDE = max(
+    1, int(os.environ.get("VLLM_OFFLOAD_SWA_STORE_STRIDE", "1") or 1)
+)
